{\fs32\b StockTool - user guide\par}
{\cf3 Everything below is also in the README next to the program. Data comes from Yahoo Finance's public endpoints and is delayed the same way their web site is.\par}
\par
{\fs26\b Watch lists\par}
The column on the left is a watch list. Click a row to select it; the chart, stats and news follow the selection. Rows show the last price and the day's change (green up, red down); a row turns amber while a price alert is active.\par
\par
{\b Add... (Ctrl+N or Ctrl+F)} opens a dialog with a search box: type a company name or part of a symbol, pick a result and the symbol and name fill in. You can also type a symbol directly. The dialog stays open so you can add several in a row; Close (or Esc) dismisses it.\par
{\b Edit... (F2, or double-click a row)} changes a ticker's symbol, name or currency. {\b Remove} deletes the selected row. {\b Move up / Move down (Ctrl+Up / Ctrl+Down)} reorder the list.\par
\par
Several lists can exist side by side: use the {\b List} menu to create, rename, delete or switch, or click the tabs that appear above the list once there is more than one. The default list cannot be renamed or deleted. Holdings, transactions, alerts and currency overrides belong to the symbol, so they follow it into every list it is in.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Symbols\par}
Symbols use Yahoo Finance notation: {\b RY.TO} for the Toronto Stock Exchange, {\b .V} for TSX Venture, a bare symbol such as {\b AAPL} for NYSE/Nasdaq, and a dash for share classes ({\b BRK-B}, {\b RCI-B.TO}).\par
Canadian mutual funds are not listed under their fund codes: TDB902 finds nothing. Search by name instead ("TD U.S. Index") and pick the fund; its symbol will look like {\b 0P000071WA.TO}. Funds have one price per day, so the 1D/5D ranges are empty and open, high, low and volume show a dash.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Chart\par}
The range buttons (or {\b Ctrl+1 ... Ctrl+8}) pick how far back the chart goes. {\b Candles} switches between a line and candlesticks (each candle shows open, high, low and close for one bar). Volume bars sit under the price. Move the mouse over the chart for a crosshair with the date, open/high/low/close and volume of that bar.\par
The small {\b trend inset} in the corner shows the last five years (or one year, see the config) at a glance; drag it anywhere inside the plot. {\b Compare} overlays every ticker in the list as percentage change over the selected range.\par
Green {\b D} markers on the chart are ex-dividend dates; the hover tooltip shows the amount.\par
{\b Benchmark index} (View menu, or the Bench button) draws the index named by {\i benchmark} in the config (the S&P/TSX Composite by default) as a dashed line, rebased so it starts where the stock starts; the tooltip then shows both percentage changes since the start of the range, which is the quickest way to see whether the stock is beating the market or just riding it.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Indicators (View menu and toolbar)\par}
The toolbar across the top of the chart area holds the same toggles as the View menu: Candles, Compare, SMA 20, SMA 50, Bollinger, RSI, the trend inset, the news pane, the benchmark index and the portfolio history. A pressed button means the option is on.\par
\par
{\b SMA - simple moving average.} The plain average of the last N closing prices, drawn as a line that follows the price. It smooths out day-to-day noise so the underlying direction is easier to see. StockTool draws {\b SMA 20} (about one trading month, orange) and {\b SMA 50} (about one quarter, purple). Price above the average means the trend over that window is up, below means down. The 20 crossing above the 50 is the classic "golden cross"; crossing below is the "death cross". Both lag by construction.\par
\par
{\b Bollinger bands.} The 20-bar average with an envelope two standard deviations above and below it, shaded on the chart. The width of the band is a volatility gauge: it squeezes when the stock is quiet and balloons when it is volatile. Price touching the upper band is stretched high relative to recent behaviour; the lower band, stretched low. Roughly 95% of closes stay inside the bands, so a close outside them is notable, and a tight squeeze often precedes a large move in either direction.\par
\par
{\b RSI - relative strength index (14).} A momentum oscillator shown in its own pane, scaled 0 to 100. It compares the size of recent up-days with recent down-days over 14 bars. {\b Above 70} is usually read as overbought (the run-up has been fast and a pullback is more likely); {\b below 30} as oversold. The dashed lines mark those levels. Divergence is the more useful signal: price making a new high while RSI makes a lower high suggests the move is losing steam.\par
\par
All three describe what has already happened; they are commonly used to time an entry within a view you already hold, not to form the view.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Holdings and portfolio\par}
{\b Ticker > Holding...} records shares held and the average cost per share. The header then shows the position's value, gain and day change, and a portfolio strip above the list totals every holding in the active list, converted into the portfolio currency (CAD by default, {\i portfolio_currency} in the config) with live exchange rates. Dividend income is estimated from the last twelve months of ex-dividend amounts.\par
{\b Ticker > Transactions...} is the more accurate alternative: enter each buy and sell with its date, number of shares and price (a sell is a negative number of shares). Shares held and average cost are then computed from the transactions (average-cost method, the same as adjusted cost base for Canadian tax purposes) and replace the manual holding; the header adds the unrealised gain, the gain realised on sales and the dividends actually received while the shares were held, and the portfolio strip totals the realised gains and dividends received across the list.\par
{\b File > Import from TD...} fills transactions or holdings from a TD Direct Investing (WebBroker) export instead of typing them: export your Activity (for buys and sells) or Holdings (for quantity and average cost) as CSV, then pick the file. A summary shows what will be written and which symbols are not yet in a watch list (a checkbox adds them to the current list). Rows already recorded are skipped, so importing the same file twice is harmless. Symbols are matched to the watch lists automatically ({\b RY} on market CA becomes {\b RY.TO}); for fund codes add a line to the {\i [symbol_map]} section of the config, e.g. TDB902=0P0000A30L. Nothing is sent anywhere and no login is involved.
{\b View > Portfolio history} (the History button) swaps the price chart for the value of the whole list over time: value as a solid line, cost base dotted, and the benchmark index dashed and rebased to the first day in view, so you can see whether the portfolio is beating the market. The range buttons pick the window and hovering shows each day's figures. The value is written once a day to portfolio-history.csv next to the config file whenever StockTool is running with holdings, so the line grows from the day you start using it.
If the data provider labels a listing in the wrong currency, set the currency in the ticker's Edit dialog.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Alerts\par}
{\b Ticker > Alerts...} sets a price above and/or below which to be told. When the price crosses a level the row turns amber, the status line says so and a notification pops from the tray icon. The alert re-arms once the price crosses back.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Stats, news and export\par}
Under the chart: open, high, low, previous close, volume, 3-month average volume, 52-week range, market cap, trailing and forward P/E and dividend yield (with the annual amount). {\b View > News pane} adds the latest headlines for the selected ticker; click one to open it in your browser. By default they come from Google News (Canadian edition) searched by the ticker's display name, so a good name such as \ldblquote Royal Bank of Canada\rdblquote  gives better results than a bare symbol; {\i news_source=yahoo} in the config switches to Yahoo's feed. {\b File > Export} writes the watch list or the current chart's bars as a CSV file.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Everything else\par}
Prices refresh on a timer ({\i refresh_seconds}) while a market in the list is open and every {\i closed_refresh_seconds} (15 minutes by default) once every exchange in the list has closed; the header says {\b Market open} or {\b Market closed} and the status line counts down to the next check. {\b F5} refreshes at any time. {\b View > Theme} picks light, dark or the Windows setting. {\b Minimize to tray} hides the window instead of minimizing it; the tray icon's tooltip lists every price, left-click restores the window, right-click offers Show / Refresh / Exit.\par
Window size, range, selected ticker, list and every toggle are remembered between runs. The configuration file is {\b stocktool.cfg}: next to the program when run from a folder you can write to, otherwise in %APPDATA%\\StockTool (Help > About shows the path). {\b Reload cfg} re-reads it after a hand edit.\par
{\b Help > Data source health...} shows how each data endpoint (chart, fundamentals, search, exchange rates, news, crumb) is doing: last HTTP status, latency, time since the last success, consecutive failures and the last error, plus how many fetches are waiting. If Yahoo rate-limits the program (HTTP 429) it backs off that endpoint for one minute, then two, four and eight on repeats; the status line and the health window say when it will retry.\par
When Yahoo cannot supply a chart at all, the same bars are fetched from {\b TMX Money} (the service behind money.tmx.com) as a fallback: daily bars only, so the 1D and 5D ranges stay empty and there are no fundamentals, but prices, charts, the benchmark and the portfolio keep working. The header shows {\b TMX Money (fallback)} while that is the case. Exchange rates for Canadian-dollar pairs fall back to the Bank of Canada's daily average. {\i fallback_provider=none} in the config turns this off.\par
\par
{\fs26\b Keyboard shortcuts\par}
F1 this guide \'b7 F5 refresh \'b7 F2 edit ticker \'b7 Ctrl+N / Ctrl+F add or search \'b7 Ctrl+Up / Ctrl+Down move ticker \'b7 Ctrl+1 ... Ctrl+8 chart range \'b7 Esc closes dialogs\par
